================================================================================
DEPENDENCY UPDATES LOG
Microsoft SQL Server to PostgreSQL Migration
Date: 2026-01-26
================================================================================

This log documents the package dependency changes for migrating from SQL Server
to PostgreSQL database connectivity.

================================================================================
PACKAGE DEPENDENCY CHANGE
================================================================================

File Modified: AdoCore.csproj
Change Type: Database Provider Package Replacement
Date of Change: 2026-01-26

OLD PACKAGE:
------------
Package Name: Microsoft.Data.SqlClient
Version: 5.1.4
Purpose: Microsoft SQL Server database connectivity provider for ADO.NET
NuGet URL: https://www.nuget.org/packages/Microsoft.Data.SqlClient/5.1.4

NEW PACKAGE:
------------
Package Name: Npgsql
Version: 8.0.1
Purpose: PostgreSQL database connectivity provider for ADO.NET
NuGet URL: https://www.nuget.org/packages/Npgsql/8.0.1

REASON FOR CHANGE:
------------------
PostgreSQL database migration - Replacing SQL Server connectivity with PostgreSQL
connectivity as part of the comprehensive database migration project.

Npgsql 8.0.1 is the latest stable version compatible with .NET 9.0 and provides:
- Full ADO.NET provider implementation for PostgreSQL
- Async/await support
- Parameter binding compatible with existing @parameter syntax
- Transaction support (BeginTransaction, Commit, Rollback)
- Connection pooling
- Compatible API surface with SqlClient for minimal code changes

UNCHANGED DEPENDENCIES:
-----------------------
The following packages remain at their current versions:
- Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration: Version 8.0.0
- Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Json: Version 8.0.0
- Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection: Version 8.0.0

These packages are database-agnostic and support both SQL Server and PostgreSQL
connectivity patterns.

COMPATIBILITY NOTES:
--------------------
1. Npgsql 8.0.1 is compatible with .NET 9.0 (current target framework)
2. ADO.NET interfaces remain consistent (IDbConnection, IDbCommand, IDataReader)
3. Parameter syntax (@paramName) is supported by Npgsql
4. Async methods (ExecuteReaderAsync, ExecuteNonQueryAsync, ExecuteScalarAsync) are fully supported
5. Transaction handling (BeginTransactionAsync, CommitAsync, RollbackAsync) is supported

MIGRATION IMPACT:
-----------------
1. Code changes required:
   - using Microsoft.Data.SqlClient → using Npgsql
   - SqlConnection → NpgsqlConnection
   - SqlCommand → NpgsqlCommand
   - SqlDataReader → NpgsqlDataReader
   - SqlParameter → NpgsqlParameter (if explicitly used)

2. Connection string format changes required:
   - SQL Server format → PostgreSQL format
   - See connection_string_updates.log for details

3. No changes required for:
   - Parameter binding (Parameters.AddWithValue)
   - Async/await patterns
   - Using statements and IDisposable patterns
   - Transaction patterns
   - Error handling patterns

VERSION SELECTION RATIONALE:
----------------------------
Npgsql 8.0.1 selected because:
- Latest stable version as of migration date
- Full .NET 9.0 support
- Mature, production-ready
- Extensive community support
- Active maintenance and security updates
- High compatibility with SqlClient API patterns

SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS:
------------------------
- Always use parameterized queries (already implemented with @parameters)
- Keep Npgsql package updated for security patches
- Review Npgsql security advisories regularly
- Use secure connection strings (encrypt sensitive data)
- Follow PostgreSQL security best practices

TESTING REQUIREMENTS:
---------------------
After this dependency change:
1. Restore packages: dotnet restore
2. Build project: dotnet build
3. Verify Npgsql package appears in bin/Debug/net9.0 directory
4. Update code to use Npgsql classes (Step 6)
5. Update connection strings (Step 7)
6. Run integration tests against PostgreSQL database
7. Verify all CRUD operations work correctly
8. Test transaction handling
9. Verify connection pooling behavior
10. Load test to ensure performance meets requirements

ROLLBACK PROCEDURE:
-------------------
If rollback is necessary:
1. Restore AdoCore.csproj from version control
2. Change Npgsql back to Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 5.1.4
3. Restore using statements and class names
4. Restore connection strings
5. Run dotnet restore and dotnet build
6. Test against SQL Server database

